issue.body:
A seed value of 0 is treated as false so both instances of lcg.setSeed fall through to the Math.round(Math.random() * m) case. As a result, you get a different randomly-selected seed each time instead of the seed you asked for.

Here's an alternative implementation that permits a seed of 0 and ensures that the seed is an integer between 0 and m-1:
```javascript
setSeed: function (val) {
  z = seed = Math.floor((val === undefined) ? Math.random() * m : val);
}
```

comment.body:
I figured it out: mask the value with an "and". There's a couple ways of doing that::
```javascript
z = seed = (val == null ? Math.random() * m : val ) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
z = seed = (val == null ? Math.random() * m : val ) & (m - 1);
```

issue.body:
The recent addition of the combine() function in Zend\Db\Sql\Select works great for doing a union of two select statements, but MySQL throws a syntax error when adding more. Specifically, it doesn't like the extra parens created in the following use case:

```php
$selectA = new Select;
$selectB = new Select;
$selectC = new Select;

$selectB->combine($selectC);

$selectA->combine($selectB);
```

creates a select statement similar to the following: 
```sql
( SELECT `foo` FROM `bar1` ) UNION ( ( SELECT `foo` FROM `bar2` ) UNION ( SELECT `foo` FROM `bar3`) )
```

See related discussion on this pull request: https://github.com/zendframework/zf2/pull/3962#issuecomment-19616900

